WEBVTT

00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:02.170
Five discs feel identical.

00:00:02.840 --> 00:00:05.010
Two show a sun and three show a moon.

00:00:05.640 --> 00:00:07.210
Mix them and draw without looking.

00:00:07.640 --> 00:00:09.210
Each disc is equally likely.

00:00:09.920 --> 00:00:12.090
Leave the first disc out when you draw the next one.

00:00:12.440 --> 00:00:13.790
How does that change your chances?

00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:18.170
The first disc is a sun with probability two fifths.

00:00:18.620 --> 00:00:21.380
After that sun comes out, only one sun remains

00:00:21.380 --> 00:00:22.510
among four discs.

00:00:23.460 --> 00:00:26.370
The chance of two suns is two fifths times one quarter.

00:00:27.020 --> 00:00:27.830
That is one tenth.

00:00:28.760 --> 00:00:31.750
Now try for one sun and one moon in either order.

00:00:32.260 --> 00:00:34.660
If a sun comes first, three of the four discs

00:00:34.660 --> 00:00:35.630
left are moons.

00:00:36.620 --> 00:00:39.200
If a moon comes first, how many of the four discs

00:00:39.200 --> 00:00:40.530
left are suns?

00:00:44.250 --> 00:00:45.960
Two suns remain after a moon.

00:00:46.550 --> 00:00:49.110
Sun then moon has probability two fifths

00:00:49.110 --> 00:00:50.220
times three quarters.

00:00:50.850 --> 00:00:53.470
Moon then sun has probability three fifths

00:00:53.470 --> 00:00:54.480
times two quarters.

00:00:55.190 --> 00:00:57.470
Each order contributes six twentieths.

00:00:57.470 --> 00:01:00.560
They cannot happen together on one draw of two discs,

00:01:00.910 --> 00:01:01.600
so add them.

00:01:02.270 --> 00:01:04.620
Twelve twentieths simplifies to three fifths.

00:01:05.530 --> 00:01:08.120
The machine now draws two discs in secret.

00:01:08.790 --> 00:01:11.680
A lamp lights exactly when at least one sun appears.

00:01:12.010 --> 00:01:13.100
You see the lamp light.

00:01:13.830 --> 00:01:16.440
What is the chance that both discs show suns?

00:01:16.730 --> 00:01:19.120
The lamp never tells you which disc came first.

00:01:22.940 --> 00:01:25.460
Label the five discs so we can tell every

00:01:25.460 --> 00:01:26.510
ordered pair apart.

00:01:27.280 --> 00:01:29.980
Five choices for the first disc, then four for the

00:01:29.980 --> 00:01:32.730
second, make twenty equally likely pairs.

00:01:33.540 --> 00:01:35.770
The lamp stays dark only for two moons.

00:01:36.460 --> 00:01:39.310
Three choices followed by two give six dark pairs.

00:01:39.920 --> 00:01:42.070
Fourteen pairs are left when the lamp lights.

00:01:43.100 --> 00:01:46.700
Only two of those fourteen pairs use both suns.

00:01:46.700 --> 00:01:47.950
Either sun can come first.

00:01:48.620 --> 00:01:50.370
Two out of fourteen is one seventh.

00:01:50.960 --> 00:01:54.200
Keep the remaining discs up to date on each draw.

00:01:54.200 --> 00:01:56.130
When a competition question gives you a clue,

00:01:56.500 --> 00:01:58.930
keep only the outcomes that could have produced it.
